#version 450
//W7: the scene composite (boundary 3.2), one invocation per full-resolution pixel. It turns vanilla's frame into the lit scene in W2's
// scene units (postmath.glsl) and assembles the other packages' exports; it redesigns none of them:
//   sky pixel (nothing wrote the depth): W4's skyColour + sunGlow, W6's sunDisc and (round, session 5) moonDisc, W4's vanillaStars
//     (vanilla's stars and square moon), the aurora (aurora.glsl, session 5), then the far clouds over it (clouds.glsl cloudMarch, W4
//     cloudOver). Without an atmosphere (W4's NONE) and without sky light (NO_SKY: the End keeps its sky box, B17) the game's pixel,
//     converted, plus the aurora; under an environmental fog W4's fog colour.
//   geometry pixel (vanilla or LOD, viewpos.glsl):
//     1. vanillaToScene (W2) of the main colour
//     2. its light: sky and block levels from the light volume or the LOD light (lightvolume.glsl pixelLight), the sky light lifted
//        where the sun reaches (RelightMath's lift), water's side sky (B12), the held light (B16); vanilla's lightmap (lightmapTex)
//        turns the levels into colours and the colour is scaled from the old light to the new (relightGain, the held gain bound)
//     3. its sky-lit share (from the lightmap) takes W5's sun factor (lightingAt, sunShadingAt; plant pixels their own plantSunTex,
//        light-source pixels none where they are light, B13), with vanilla's baked face shade divided out under the sun weight (W5 replaces it), the
//        light colour (slots 22/23, B15) and the sky exposure; the block-lit share keeps its colour and takes the block exposure (B14).
//        Translucent pixels (water, ice, glass, slime) have no face shade divided out: they are a texel blended over what lies behind
//     4. water: W6's waterColour over the length the view travels through it, lit by what a white texel here receives
//     5. W4's haze over the view distance (hazeAmount: the lifted sky light given to sunShadingAt, water its unlifted one as W6's
//        reflection takes it, LOD pixels full), then the far clouds in front of it
//     6. alpha: W6's reflectionMark of W5's translucent kind (0 for sky, and everywhere when the lighting pass did not run)
//   vanilla's own clouds (B3) take no lighting at all; the light debug views 1..3 (B18) replace the colour.
// W10: the ambient occlusion (aoTex, binding 13, ao.glsl's aoAt) at slot 10 .x's strength darkens only the sky light's ambient part
// (the sun shading's level without direct sun) and the lightmap's no-light floor; never the direct sun, block light or the sky.
// Final fix R1 (B20): every exposure added here (NO_SKY_, UNDERWATER_, DAY_BLOCK_, NIGHT_SKY_, OVERCAST_, UNDERGROUND_SKY_EXPOSURE) is
// mix(1, E, look), look = W4's slot 29 .x (the sun-shading setting / 100); they do not depend on whether the lighting pass ran, and the
// sky-light ones skip only light an active light volume does not know yet (a section still loading; with the volume off every pixel takes them). Final fix R2: under water is slot 29 .y (the
// camera's fluid), not the fog colour.
// 2026-11-09 (session 2b): with the camera in water every pixel, sky included, is then seen through the water (underwaterView,
// underwater.glsl): what it shows (at UNDERWATER_EXPOSURE) through the water between, and where the view rises to the surface (slots
// 35..38) Snell's window onto the sky of the air above (slots 39..45, W4's struct Atmosphere as if the camera were in the open air) or
// the land behind the surface (the depth before the translucent terrain, binding 17), rippled, and the water mirrored outside it.
// SceneCompositeMath mirrors every const and the per-pixel combination here (SceneCompositeTest): change both together.
layout(local_size_x = 8, local_size_y = 8) in;

